Q: Page vs Slice in Spring Data JPA? Does Spring support cursor pagination?

Both are pagination result types. Default strategy is **offset-based** (`LIMIT` / `OFFSET`).

Important correction:
`jpaRepository.findAll(pageable)` returns **`Page`**.
For `Slice`, declare the repository method return type as `Slice<...>`.

────────────────────────────────────────
🟢 1️⃣ Page

Page<User> page = userRepository.findAll(pageable);
// or: Page<User> findByStatus(String status, Pageable pageable);

What it does:
1) Main data query (LIMIT/OFFSET)
2) Extra **COUNT(*)** query for totals

Gives:
- content
- totalElements
- totalPages
- number / size
- hasNext(), hasPrevious(), etc.

⚠️ Cost: count query can be expensive on huge tables.

Use when: UI needs “Page 3 of 40” / total count.

────────────────────────────────────────
🟢 2️⃣ Slice

Slice<User> findByStatus(String status, Pageable pageable);

What it does:
- Runs data query (typically asks for **pageSize + 1** rows)
- Does **NOT** run COUNT(*)
- Infers hasNext() from whether an extra row exists

⚡ Faster than Page on large datasets (no total count).

Does NOT give totalElements / totalPages.

Use when: infinite scroll, “Load more”, mobile feeds.

────────────────────────────────────────
Page vs Slice

| Feature        | Page                | Slice              |
| -------------- | ------------------- | ------------------ |
| Count query    | Yes (extra)         | No                 |
| totalElements  | Yes                 | No                 |
| hasNext()      | Yes                 | Yes                |
| Speed (large)  | Slower              | Faster             |
| Best for       | Classic page UI     | Infinite scroll    |
| Method return  | `Page<T>`           | `Slice<T>`         |

────────────────────────────────────────
🚨 Cursor / keyset pagination in Spring?

Default Spring Data JPA pagination = **offset** (`ORDER BY ... LIMIT ... OFFSET ...`).

True cursor/keyset pagination is **not** the same as Page/Slice out of the box.
You usually write a custom query:

@Query("""
SELECT u FROM User u
WHERE u.id > :lastId
ORDER BY u.id ASC
""")
List<User> findNext(@Param("lastId") Long lastId, Pageable pageable);

Or manually:
WHERE (created_at, id) > (:lastCreatedAt, :lastId) ORDER BY created_at, id

Why cursor?
OFFSET on page 50,000 is slow (DB still skips many rows).
Keyset uses indexed “seek” from last seen key → stable & faster for deep pages.

(Newer Spring Data also has scrolling APIs in recent versions, but interview-safe answer:
default is offset Page/Slice; cursor is custom keyset.)

────────────────────────────────────────
🔥 Interview answer

“Spring Data JPA defaults to offset pagination with LIMIT/OFFSET. Page runs an extra COUNT and exposes totals;
Slice skips COUNT and only tells if a next page exists, better for infinite scroll.
For very large/deep pagination I use custom cursor/keyset queries instead of big OFFSET values.”
